    description: "Owner request 2026-11-13 (\"ok yes please trackerize\"), raised while a full smart-test run was in flight for PR work on WI-jolov / WI-tukag.\n\nOBSERVED. That change touched four package source files. coverage-select refused to narrow: \"narrow REFUSED: import-time code changed: discovery.py (<module>); discovery.py (FileIndex); profile.py (<module>); sketch.py (<module>)\". The run fell back to the import graph: 870 dependent files, 1,247 test files, about 14 minutes estimated. The import-time edits were: a name added to an existing `from .discovery import ...` line (profile.py, sketch.py); two new module-level assignments of literals to new names (discovery.py); and, in the FileIndex class body, a new property and one more entry in `__slots__`.\n\nWHAT EXISTS, AND WHO DECIDED IT. selection_index.narrowing_blockers refuses narrowing whenever a coverable source file has a changed <module> block or class body (WI-zunug-mikoz-furut-bumoh-dahom-pijut-vohuj-ginon, PR #2129). That was an owner decision of 2026-11-10 (fix (a) on that item), for a sound reason: import-time code runs under no test's coverage context, so a test that only imports the file has no row for it, and narrowing would read the missing row as \"irrelevant\". Measured there: the rule refuses on 93 of 113 commits that modify package source (82%). ADR-0010 states the same. This item proposes a REFINEMENT of that ruling; until the owner rules on it, the 11-10 ruling stands.\n\nTHE IDEA. Some import-time changes cannot change what an already-existing test does. If every changed import-time block of a file differs from its previous version only by such statements, the file need not block narrowing.\n\nWHAT IS NOT SAFE (so \"additive\" is not the criterion). Each of these is an added statement that changes behaviour for a test that only imports the file:\n- a new `import X` / `from X import y` of a module this file did not import before: X's import can fail (missing dependency, circular import) and can have side effects. This project fills registries by import side effect (analyzers, linkers, def/use extractors).\n- a new name that shadows a builtin or a name the module already resolves another way, or that changes what `from module import *`, `__all__`, `dir(module)` or `getattr(module, ...)` consumers see. Several gates in this repository enumerate module contents.\n- anything in a class body that changes the class: a method that overrides a base method, a dataclass field (changes the constructor), a `__slots__` entry, a decorator.\n- an assignment whose right-hand side calls something.\n\nCANDIDATE SAFE SUBSET (to be measured, then ruled on by the owner):\n(i) a new top-level assignment of a literal (or empty container) to a name the module neither bound nor referenced before;\n(ii) one more name in an existing `from M import ...` statement, where the file already imported M and the name was not bound before;\n(iii) possibly: a new method or property on a class, when no base class and no subclass in the tree defines that name. This is the weakest of the three and needs the class hierarchy.\nA failed import of a new NAME under (ii) raises for every importer alike, so any one kept test that imports the file shows it; the keep set must be shown to hold one.\n\nMEASURE BEFORE BUILDING (the ceiling may be small).\n1. Over the window WI-zunug used (the last 400 commits on dev, 113 of which modified package source), or a fresh window of the same size: for how many refused commits is EVERY import-time change inside (i), (i)+(ii), (i)+(ii)+(iii)? Report per subset. The change that prompted this item would still be refused under (i)+(ii), because of the FileIndex class body; only (iii) lifts it.\n2. For the commits each subset would newly narrow: how many test files would be dropped, and how much wall time.\n3. Soundness on the smart-test bed (scripts/smart-test-bed, the mutation oracle WI-zunug used): add an edit of each kind and show `narrow` drops no MUST-select test; add at least one edit of each UNSAFE kind above and show it is still refused.\n\nDONE WHEN. The measurement is posted; the owner has ruled which subset, if any, is admitted; and, if one is: narrowing_blockers classifies an import-time change by comparing the old and new block (statement-level, from the AST), the refusal message names the statement that blocked, unit tests cover each admitted and each refused kind with a mutation control, the bed scores no dropped MUST, and ADR-0010's \"about four in five are refused\" figure is re-measured.\n\nGENERALITY. The rule is about Python import-time semantics of any package source file in this repository; it must not be keyed to particular files or names.\n\nNOT VERIFIED. That (iii) can be decided cheaply (it needs every subclass in the tree). How the block hash stores enough of the previous block to diff statements: today it may hold only a hash, in which case the previous text has to come from git."  # 0773
